{"content_hash": "f9a82e691fe32754", "version_id": 424, "article_id": 43, "level_code": "en_b2", "lang": "en", "level": 4, "level_label": "B2 中高级", "title": "NHS waiting list grows as winter pressures hit early", "title_zh": "NHS等候名单增长，冬季压力提前到来", "has_translation": true, "topic": "health", "source": "bbc_health", "source_url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cxnv03e9nmp4o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1540683153640-c28d7f6810f0?ixid=M3wxMDk3Mzk1fDB8MXxzZWFyY2h8N3x8b21pbm91cyUyMHNpZ25zJTIwd2ludGVyfGVufDB8MHx8fDE3OTE1OTQ0Mzd8MA&ixlib=rb-4.1.0&w=1600&q=75&fm=jpg&fit=max", "provider": "unsplash", "credit": "Photo by Shawn Bagley on Unsplash", "author": "Shawn Bagley", "credit_url": "https://unsplash.com/@utahdesertguy?utm_source=daily-english-news&utm_medium=referral", "source": "Unsplash", "source_url": "https://unsplash.com/photos/road-sign-near-pine-trees-bUADDFi1Qs0?utm_source=daily-english-news&utm_medium=referral", "fallback": {"type": "gradient", "from": "hsl(352, 42%, 62%)", "to": "hsl(30, 46%, 48%)", "label": "HEALTH", "seed": "b128cdf1738d"}}, "published_date": "2026-10-08", "word_count": 208, "reading_minutes": 1.0, "lead": "Senior health leaders have warned of 'ominous signs' that winter pressures are already affecting the NHS in England. New data shows the waiting list for routine hospital treatment rose for the second month in a row.", "lead_zh": "高级卫生领导人警告称，有“不祥迹象”表明冬季压力已经影响到英格兰的NHS。新数据显示，常规医院治疗的等候名单连续第二个月上升。", "preview": "An estimated 7.37 million treatments were waiting to be carried out at the end of August, relating to 6.24 million patients. These numbers a", "preview_zh": "截至8月底，估计有737万次治疗在等待进行，涉及624万名患者。这些数字是自2025年10月以来的最高水平，引发了人们对NHS能否在2029年前实现政府目标——即在18周内治疗92%的患者——的质疑。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 414, "url": "https://pub-aba43a6fb1db4dc08fede1dbc81f3241.r2.dev/audio/43/en_b2.mp3", "duration": 90.425, "size_bytes": 546336, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural", "word_count": 240, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@fc48a26/content/data/versions/424.json", "paragraphs": ["An estimated 7.37 million treatments were waiting to be carried out at the end of August, relating to 6.24 million patients. These numbers are the highest since October 2025 and have raised questions over whether the NHS will meet a government target of treating 92% of patients within 18 weeks by 2029.", "The rise comes despite the NHS delivering record levels of planned care over the summer. A record 4.7 million planned treatments, procedures and appointments took place across June, July and August. However, A&Es and ambulance services also saw a record number of patients for September.", "Professor Frankie Swords, NHS England's national medical director, said staff were already seeing early signs of pressure, with more patients coming to A&E with flu and other respiratory viruses than usual. Health Secretary Yvette Cooper praised staff but warned that pressure would increase again as winter approaches.", "The NHS Alliance said the government is unlikely to achieve its promise of hitting the 18-week waiting time target by 2029. The Health Foundation said progress on cutting waits had stalled, with referrals rising faster than treatments. In September, 74.4% of A&E patients were seen within four hours, down from 75.0% in August, and 50,582 people waited over 12 hours from decision to admit."], "paragraphs_zh": ["截至8月底，估计有737万次治疗在等待进行，涉及624万名患者。这些数字是自2025年10月以来的最高水平，引发了人们对NHS能否在2029年前实现政府目标——即在18周内治疗92%的患者——的质疑。", "尽管NHS在夏季提供了创纪录的计划护理水平，但等候名单仍上升。6月、7月和8月期间，共进行了创纪录的470万次计划治疗、手术和预约。然而，9月份急诊室和救护车服务也接待了创纪录数量的患者。", "NHS英格兰国家医疗主任弗兰基·斯沃兹教授表示，工作人员已经看到压力的早期迹象，因流感和其它呼吸道病毒到急诊室就诊的患者比往年同期更多。卫生大臣伊薇特·库珀赞扬了工作人员，但警告说随着冬季临近，压力将再次增加。", "NHS联盟表示，政府不太可能实现到2029年达到18周等候时间目标的承诺。健康基金会表示，减少等候时间的进展已经停滞，转诊人数上升速度快于治疗速度。9月份，74.4%的急诊患者在四小时内就诊，低于8月份的75.0%，并且有50,582人从决定收治到实际收治等待超过12小时。"], "body": "An estimated 7.37 million treatments were waiting to be carried out at the end of August, relating to 6.24 million patients. These numbers are the highest since October 2025 and have raised questions over whether the NHS will meet a government target of treating 92% of patients within 18 weeks by 2029.\n\nThe rise comes despite the NHS delivering record levels of planned care over the summer. A record 4.7 million planned treatments, procedures and appointments took place across June, July and August. However, A&Es and ambulance services also saw a record number of patients for September.\n\nProfessor Frankie Swords, NHS England's national medical director, said staff were already seeing early signs of pressure, with more patients coming to A&E with flu and other respiratory viruses than usual. Health Secretary Yvette Cooper praised staff but warned that pressure would increase again as winter approaches.\n\nThe NHS Alliance said the government is unlikely to achieve its promise of hitting the 18-week waiting time target by 2029. The Health Foundation said progress on cutting waits had stalled, with referrals rising faster than treatments. In September, 74.4% of A&E patients were seen within four hours, down from 75.0% in August, and 50,582 people waited over 12 hours from decision to admit.", "body_zh": "截至8月底，估计有737万次治疗在等待进行，涉及624万名患者。这些数字是自2025年10月以来的最高水平，引发了人们对NHS能否在2029年前实现政府目标——即在18周内治疗92%的患者——的质疑。\n\n尽管NHS在夏季提供了创纪录的计划护理水平，但等候名单仍上升。6月、7月和8月期间，共进行了创纪录的470万次计划治疗、手术和预约。然而，9月份急诊室和救护车服务也接待了创纪录数量的患者。\n\nNHS英格兰国家医疗主任弗兰基·斯沃兹教授表示，工作人员已经看到压力的早期迹象，因流感和其它呼吸道病毒到急诊室就诊的患者比往年同期更多。卫生大臣伊薇特·库珀赞扬了工作人员，但警告说随着冬季临近，压力将再次增加。\n\nNHS联盟表示，政府不太可能实现到2029年达到18周等候时间目标的承诺。健康基金会表示，减少等候时间的进展已经停滞，转诊人数上升速度快于治疗速度。9月份，74.4%的急诊患者在四小时内就诊，低于8月份的75.0%，并且有50,582人从决定收治到实际收治等待超过12小时。", "text": "An estimated 7.37 million treatments were waiting to be carried out at the end of August, relating to 6.24 million patients. These numbers are the highest since October 2025 and have raised questions over whether the NHS will meet a government target of treating 92% of patients within 18 weeks by 2029. The rise comes despite the NHS delivering record levels of planned care over the summer. A record 4.7 million planned treatments, procedures and appointments took place across June, July and August. However, A&Es and ambulance services also saw a record number of patients for September. Professor Frankie Swords, NHS England's national medical director, said staff were already seeing early signs of pressure, with more patients coming to A&E with flu and other respiratory viruses than usual. Health Secretary Yvette Cooper praised staff but warned that pressure would increase again as winter approaches. The NHS Alliance said the government is unlikely to achieve its promise of hitting the 18-week waiting time target by 2029. The Health Foundation said progress on cutting waits had stalled, with referrals rising faster than treatments. In September, 74.4% of A&E patients were seen within four hours, down from 75.0% in August, and 50,582 people waited over 12 hours from decision to admit.", "vocab": [{"word": "waiting list", "pos": "noun", "zh": "等候名单", "note": "常用于医疗、服务等场景"}, {"word": "routine", "pos": "adj", "zh": "常规的", "note": ""}, {"word": "treatment", "pos": "noun", "zh": "治疗", "note": ""}, {"word": "ominous", "pos": "adj", "zh": "不祥的", "note": "常形容迹象、预兆"}, {"word": "pressure", "pos": "noun", "zh": "压力", "note": "此处指医疗系统承受的压力"}, {"word": "record", "pos": "adj", "zh": "创纪录的", "note": "也可作名词，意为纪录"}, {"word": "elective", "pos": "adj", "zh": "择期的", "note": "指非紧急的、计划好的医疗"}, {"word": "respiratory", "pos": "adj", "zh": "呼吸的", "note": "常与病毒、疾病搭配"}, {"word": "target", "pos": "noun", "zh": "目标", "note": ""}, {"word": "stall", "pos": "verb", "zh": "停滞", "note": "指进展停止"}, {"word": "referral", "pos": "noun", "zh": "转诊", "note": "指医生将病人转给专科"}, {"word": "admit", "pos": "verb", "zh": "收治入院", "note": "此处指允许病人住院"}], "title_original": "'Ominous signs' of winter pressures as NHS waiting list grows", "summary_original": "NHS winter pressures appear to be building already, with rises in waiting times for planned surgery.", "body_original": "'Ominous signs' of winter pressures as NHS waiting list grows\nBy\nDominic Hughes\nHealth correspondent\nPublished\n8 October 2026\nThere are \"ominous signs\" that winter pressures are already affecting the NHS in England, according to senior health leaders.\nThe warning comes as new data reveals the waiting list for routine hospital treatment in England rose for the second month in a row.\nAn estimated 7.37 million treatments were waiting to be carried out at the end of August, relating to 6.24 million patients.\nThe numbers are the highest since October 2025 and have raised questions over whether the NHS will meet a government target of treating 92% of patients within 18 weeks by 2029.\nThe rise in waiting lists comes despite the NHS in England delivering what were described as record levels of planned, elective care over the summer, while demand for services also reached its highest level ever.\nA record 4.7 million planned treatments, procedures and appointments occurred across June, July and August – more than in any previous summer.\nBut both A&Es and ambulance services also saw a record number of patients for the month of September.\nNHS England's national medical director, Professor Frankie Swords, warned that despite the progress delivered by frontline teams, winter pressure was already building.\n\"Staff are already seeing the ominous early signs of pressure, with more patients coming to A&E with flu and other respiratory viruses than usual for this time of year, and unfortunately many people are still waiting longer than we would like them to,\" she said.\nHealth and Social Care Secretary, Yvette Cooper said the NHS has been dealing with summer pressures as well as winter pressures.\n\"It is a huge tribute to doctors, nurses and staff across the NHS that alongside dealing with unprecedented levels of A&E attendances and ambulance call-outs, they also managed to deliver record numbers of elective appointments, procedures and treatments.\n\"Step by step, our NHS has been increasing its delivery and performance, making a real difference to patients' lives even as demand is growing.\n\"But with winter now approaching, pressure on our NHS is set to increase again and I would encourage everyone who is eligible for the flu jab and other vaccines to get themselves protected against preventable illnesses, and help relieve the workload on the NHS too.\"\n\"Relentless growth in demand for care\"\nThe NHS Alliance, which represents hospitals, says the government in England is not going to achieve its manifesto promise of hitting the 18-week hospital waiting time target by the end of the parliament in 2029.\nThat 18-week target hasn't been hit for a decade.\nA separate analysis by the Health Foundation think-tank said progress on cutting waiting times had stalled over the past year, with referrals on to the waiting list rising at a much quicker rate than the number of patients being treated.\nSir Ciarán Devane, the chief executive of the NHS Alliance which represents NHS trusts, said: \"The NHS continues to respond to record demand for care, but the relentless growth in demand for services is putting NHS staff under real pressure.\"\nFrancesca Cavallaro, of the Health Foundation, said: \"Thanks to the hard work of staff, the NHS has made progress in bringing down waiting times, but a rise in referrals mean that the service is having to run faster just to stand still and the prospects of meeting the government's pledge are receding.\n\"Andy Burnham's government have yet to set out whether they will give the 18-week target the same priority as it had under Keir Starmer, but our analysis shows the pledge is becoming increasingly hard to deliver.\"\n\"Not just numbers, but vulnerable people\"\nThere will be concern too that the pressures of a long, hot summer have merged almost seamlessly into the pressures associated with the winter months.\nMore patients, including children, have been arriving at A&E departments with flu and other respiratory viruses.\nThe number of patients seen within four hours in A&Es last month fell to 74.4%, down from 75.0% in August.\nThe number of people waiting more than 12 hours in A&E departments from a decision to admit to actually being admitted – what is known as \"corridor care\" – stood at 50,582 in September, up from 44,951 in August.\nThat has alarmed Dr Vicky Price, president of the Society for Acute Medicine.\n\"A 13% year-on-year rise in 12-hour emergency department waits as winter approaches should set alarm bells ringing.\n\"More than 50,000 patients waited over 12 hours from the decision to admit to admission in September - 1,686 people every day.\n\"These are not just numbers.\n\"They represent vulnerable people who are waiting far too long for a bed and staff trying to deliver safe care in overcrowded emergency departments.\"\nThe government says the 18-week mark could still be achieved, saying it was investing in NHS buildings and equipment to make services more productive.\nIt also pointed to disruption to care that was caused by the strikes by resident doctors, a dispute which has now been settled.\nBut the strain that services are feeling in September, before winter has even really got going, will be a cause for concern among NHS leaders and their political bosses alike.\nGet in touch\nHave you been on an NHS waiting list for a long time? Share your experience\nContact form\nContact form\nRelated topics\nNHS\nEngland\nHealth\nNHS England\nWaiting times\nMore on this story\nRise in people waiting for NHS treatment for fourth month in a row\nPublished\n17 September\nFears of two-tier health system as more turn to private care, says watchdog\nPublished\n16 March\nI'm on six different NHS waiting lists - it's taking over my life\nPublished\n23 April", "fetched_at": "2026-10-10T01:07:16.505883", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "An", "s": 0.